{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"d2783eed-8b6b-474b-813c-d25769949a91","status":"SUCCESS","response":"Here is the requestedHere is the requested analysis and fix.\n\n- **Cause** – The `similarity` function returned a float. DynamoDB does not accept native float values, so the first publish (empty table, no `similar` scores) succeeded, while the second publish attempted to store floats in the `similar` attribute and raised `TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.`  \n- **Fix** – Convert the similarity score to an integer (percentage) before storing it. The `similarity` implementation in `src/app.py` has been updated accordingly.  \n- **Relevant parts of the colleague’s note** – The note’s description of the float‑type error, the fact that it only appears on the second write when data exists, and the recommended fix of converting scores to `int`/`Decimal` all directly apply.\n\nThe full explanation is in the generated artifact:\n\n[ANSWER.md](file:///Users/valentinakoniukhova/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/d2783eed-8b6b-474b-813c-d25769949a91/ANSWER.md)\n","duration_seconds":34.092326,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":13536,"output_tokens":2547,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":75968,"total_tokens":16083}}}
